r said if people can recite the 300  items in the book of songs yet still  fail to make use of that knowledge when  given an official position or when sent  abroad on diplomatic missions  what is the use of studying even though  they may have learned so much  what is the use of it to them so mere  recitation we might even say following  see right Mills mere technical ability  that is not enough there needs to be  real commitment the students Confucius  admirers need to be all in but let's  take another look at Confucius's  commitment to study that embraces one's  whole life this brief passage is as  succinct as it gets and yet it speaks to  Confucius his perspective that there is  no distance from deep learning it does  not stand offish it is immersed  intellect 620 the master said knowing  something is not as good as loving it  loving it is not as good as savoring it  think about the implications of this  perspective especially if you've come to  think of learning as something that  takes place from a neutral or objective  position  confucius seems to be saying something  else here and i don't think that it  means that one will be mired in sloppy  subjectivity rather i think that he is  saying that you have to love what you do  and how you learn and you need to savor  the process above all as Searight mills  might say such learning is a way of life  not a subject of study  another entry in the Analects gives us a  further perspective here Confucius  stresses that he will not let up until  he has gotten to the bottom of a problem  no matter who asks it he will just keep  thinking letting it drum into his  thoughts percolating really take a  listen handle x98 the master said do I  have great knowledge no I do not yet if  an ordinary rustic asks me a question  and I have no answer I will pursue it  from beginning to end until I have gain  deeper insight think about times when  you have been completely into learning  something you know you have just think  and when you were you likely pursuit it  from beginning to end until you gain  deeper insight okay and then what did  you do well you probably taught it to  others you may not have done this in a  formal way  but you conveyed it to others this is  what deep reflection for Confucius means  you craft an understanding on your own  and sometimes with others and then you  pass it on to others let's let Confucius  wrap up this idea for us and like seven  - the master said to persist in gaining  knowledge to study without flagging and  to teach without tiring are these not  the qualities I have  Confucius was a learner and a teacher he  wanted others to be too and his lesson  has persisted for 2,500 years I have  been delighted to discover how  Confucius's ideas on the ethical and  social imagination resonate with people  today and how much eagerness there is on  the part of students to explore them  when I first signed on to teach this  course I decided that a good way to work  through the key ideas surrounding  Confucius and the Analects would be to  teach a class about the topic that's  what I always do when I want to explore  something so I set out to teach  Confucius and the world the very next  semester at my college the college is  quite small and deeply committed to  discussion of key texts so we keep  classes small to even our larger classes  tend to have no more than 25 students  and the target for our history 150  introductory seminars that was the  audience I tapped for Confucius and the  world was 15 students well 40 signed up  without a prompt just a paragraph long  blurb in the registration materials  telling prospective students that we  would like to make sense of Confucius  and his dense little treatise the  Analects when I saw the enrollment  number I asked the Dean if I could offer  a second section so that we could have  back-to-back seminars instead of a big  lecture class and now there are from  just one semester forty students at one  small College who know a great deal  about the philosophy of Confucius and  how it could change their lives they  have tapped into the creative  imagination that Confucius taught and  see right Mills taught many centuries  later and now they are ready to tackle  even larger questions about where  Confucius is thinking fits into a  complex Chinese tradition as well as an  extraordinarily different Western  tradition it is exactly this goal that  drives these lectures about Confucius in  the Analects and it is certainly not  only at one small college in Wisconsin  that such transformations are taking  place  from the University of Hawaii to Harvard  and from Montana to Texas and all  through North America Europe and the  rest of the world courses in Chinese  philosophy have produced robust  enrollment figures even at a time when  many people Bellone the sheer  practicality of interests among students  something is going on we would do best  to listen to that hunger for  understanding  it wasn't always this way some time ago  I found myself in a conversation with a  grad student in philosophy at one of the  elite philosophy departments in the  United States I explained that I was in  the process of integrating Confucius's  philosophical teachings that was the  precise phrase I used in just something  that I found useful in and beyond my  studies of Chinese history and  anthropology I was completely unprepared  for his response though Confucius he  snorted and that Analects then he paused  Confucius has nothing to do with  philosophy it's just not philosophy  maybe it is a kind of Asian wisdom he  voiced that phrase with utter derision  it is not philosophy though  I wish I could say that this student who  is soon to become a professor was alone  in his opinion about the great Chinese  thinker one of the best books about  philosophy that I have ever read is  Brian McGee's memoir confessions of a  philosopher although McGee's book is  squarely about the Western philosophical  tradition he raises an important point  for all of us early on you see Magee  feels that the profession of philosophy  has failed us and exactly when we need  it most  he describes his own experience studying  philosophy at Oxford the 1950s and being  disgusted with what he regarded as the  tiny little questions about how we use  language favoured by analytical  philosophers of that era it wasn't until  he undertook a year of study at Yale in  the 1960s that in reading Immanuel  Kant's three critiques the critique of  Pure Reason the critique of practical  reason and the critique of judgment that  McGee found a philosopher who asked big  philosophical questions on what life in  our world is all about  but here's the problem Westerners tend  to think about big questions through one  of two lenses one of my colleagues has  spoken of these lenses as the microscope  and the telescope and I like his  characterization sometimes we  contemplate the great structures of the  universe or the vast sweep of human  history through our interpretive  telescopes at other times we study the  tiny structures that shape our  microscopic knowledge from DNA to  subatomic particles but both lenses  embody the Western emphasis on logic  rationality and a highly individualistic  view of the world confucius certainly  doesn't seem to be looking at the world  through those lenses or to be asking the  big questions of which brian mcgee spoke  Immanuel Kant asked how we know what we  think we know he asked about our  aesthetic standards and he argued for  uncompromising ethical standards how  could Confucius ever compete with that  well I think he does a colleague and I  were talking about this matter recently  at a big comparative philosophy  conference in Hawaii we both struggle to  find a way to sum up with the 500  entries in the Analects are all about  and then just as our bus pulled up to  our destination we both blurted out as  solid and answer as I have ever heard  they're about getting good at life  exactly Confucius's and like to do deal  with the big questions they don't get  any bigger than how we should live our  lives with and among others  but I freely admit that early in my  career I had my doubts about Confucius  when I started out in Chinese studies  the consensus at least among most  students and many professors was that  Confucius was an impractical rule making  boar the general view was that he had  little to teach us in modern society and  that the Analects was only a value for  what it could tell us about China's  imperial past the idea that Confucius  could be the carrier of a vital message  of a way of knowing and acting in the  world struck us as nonsense we just  weren't buying it decades ago when  people asked me about Confucius I told  them that I saw much more subtlety and  spontaneity in the teachings of  enigmatic thinkers who mocked Confucius  and his followers with great relish in  the centuries during and after Confucius  lived it was another scholar one who  once had also been skeptical of  Confucius who changed my mind the  philosopher Herbert finger ette wrote a  splendid book called Confucius the  secular as sacred and it led many of us  in Western academic circles to rethink  our approaches to Confucius in the  Analects I first read finger at spoke  right after college just as I began  haltingly at first to read the Analects  in Chinese here is how finger Rhett  describes his own discovery of the sage  when I began to read Confucius I found  him to be a prosaic and parochial  moralizer his collected sayings the  Analects seemed to me an archaic  irrelevance later and with increasing  force I found him a thinker with  profound insight and with an imaginative  vision of man equal in its grandeur to  any I know I was floored by this  statement of Confucius as potential  relevance not just to something I  thought of his traditional Chinese  philosophy but to my own life as well  suddenly I realized that my readings the  Analects have been mechanical that in  --see right Mills's language I was just  going through the motions thinking like  a technician when I first read Confucius  is Analects thing read continues  explaining his own changed perspective  increasingly I have become convinced  that Confucius can be a teacher to us  today a major teacher not one who merely  gives us a highly exotic perspective on  the ideas or any current he tells us  things not being said elsewhere things  needing to be said he has a new lesson  to teach  what finger Rhett touches upon is the  thoroughly engaged nature of knowledge  and action in Confucius's Analects there  is no telescope no microscope no  rational and dispassionate aloofness for  subjects at hand Confucian engagement  differs radically from many Western  ideals of disengage study from the  outside you must be all in to comprehend  the Analects and the lessons of which  finger Rhett speaks you must enter the  conversation  what conversation am i speaking of  flex exchange of questions and answers  assertions and observations challenges  and retorts that make up the gentle  pedagogical rhythms of the Analects  it welcomes the reader who is willing to  engage with it but learning to do so can  admittedly be challenging let's look at  some examples of the kinds of exchanges  I'm talking about so you can see how you  might enter the conversation of the  Analects yourself a large number of  entries and Confucius's Analects set up  a special kind of social scene it is one  in which someone sometimes dukes and  territorial governors other times his  own students ask him a question his  answers can help us to enter the growing  dialogue and I would urge you to listen  for these social interactions as we  proceed Analects 17/8 the master said  Zulu have you heard of the six negative  qualities in the six positive qualities  no I haven't replied Zulu well sit down  then said the master I will instruct you  wanting to be consummate in conduct but  not wanting to study that is a first  flaw and it will lead to fatuousness  wanting knowledge but not wanting to  study that is a second flaw and it will  lead to rootlessness  the master continues wanting to be true  to one's word but not wanting to study  that is a third flaw and it leads to  impairment wanting to be upright but not  wanting to study that is a fourth flaw  and it leads to impudence wanting to be  brave without wanting to study that is a  fifth flaw and it leads to disorder  finally wanting to be solid and firm but  not wanting to study that is a sixth  flaw and it leads to fool hardiness in  many ways this kind of entry teaches the  reader or listener how to enter the  conversation  I'm reminded of an anecdote in one of TS  Eliot's essays he said of his form of  highly innovative poetry that the poems  themselves could teach the reader how to  read the poetry in other words reading  it helped one to learn that something  profound new and different was going on  in the passage you just heard  Confucius uses a series of ordinary  words and contrast them with study or  not studying  people who confronted Confucius's  teachings during the masters lifetime as  well as in the decades and centuries  that followed we're hearing something  different from the ways they used those  words  Confucius used concepts in new and  sometimes startling ways interactions  like the one you just heard are an  example of Confucius teaching his  listeners how to think creatively  imaginatively and to continue to do so  for themselves and for others  if you're still struggling to grasp how  the text of the Analects works don't  despair there will be many more  opportunities in this course to  understand it and rest assured that  you're not alone I know plenty of people  who earnestly wish to understand  Confucius's message and have tried often  repeatedly to do so a close friend and  colleague of mine confided recently that  every few years I pick up a translation  of the Analects and try to read it again  I know that I want to grasp what  Confucius has to say but I just give up  after a few dozen pages I can't seem to  understand where it is going no amount  of hard work or perseverance ever makes  a difference  I can't just muscle my way through the  text  Analects are indeed difficult to  penetrate at the outset but if you can  master the essentials of reading them  you will find yourself greatly rewarded  and your life enriched as many  persistent readers have discovered to do  so requires that you enter the world of  the text in a way that is different from  the one in which most of us were trained  imagine what it was like in a much  earlier era when information at the  click of a button was a thing of utter  fantasy imagine a world in which the  gift of a book could change your life  forever imagine a world in which you  didn't just read but read reread and  read again and lived a book it is so far  from the way that we think today that we  need to remind ourselves of the power  that a single book can contain  Confucius's Analects contains that kind  of transformative power and you can  experience it for yourself if you  approach the book in the right way it  must be read reread and lived
 

